        <a href="#update">Update</a>
      </nav>

      <div class="doc-body">
        <section id="install">
          <h2 class="h3">Install</h2>
          <h3>Quick install</h3>
          <div class="install-cmds">
            <div class="install-cmd">
              <span class="install-cmd__os">macOS · Linux</span>
              <button class="cmd" type="button" data-copy="bash <(curl -fssL getmeshtalk.raymont.dev/sh)">
                <span class="cmd__prompt">$</span>
                <code>bash &lt;(curl -fssL getmeshtalk.raymont.dev/sh)</code>
                <span class="cmd__feedback" aria-live="polite" hidden>Copied!</span>
              </button>
            </div>
            <div class="install-cmd">
              <span class="install-cmd__os">Windows · PowerShell</span>
              <button class="cmd" type="button" data-copy="irm getmeshtalk.raymont.dev/ps1 | iex">
                <span class="cmd__prompt">PS&gt;</span>
                <code>irm getmeshtalk.raymont.dev/ps1 | iex</code>
                <span class="cmd__feedback" aria-live="polite" hidden>Copied!</span>
              </button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>This downloads the release for your platform and installs to <code>~/.local/bin</code> on Linux and macOS, or <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\MeshTalk</code> on Windows.</p>
          <h3>Options</h3>
          <div class="code-block"><span class="k">--non-interactive</span>   accept all defaults
<span class="k">--version TAG</span>      install a specific release
<span class="k">--install-dir DIR</span>  choose where it lands
<span class="k">--prerelease</span>       include pre-releases
<span class="k">--dry-run</span>          preview without installing
<span class="k">--uninstall</span>        remove MeshTalk</div>
          <h3>Manual install</h3>
          <p>Download the <code>.tar.gz</code> for your platform from the latest release, extract it, and run <code>./meshtalk</code>. The archive carries everything: no Python, Bun, or package manager at runtime.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="quick-start">
          <h2 class="h3">Quick start</h2>
          <div class="code-block"><span class="c"># launch the TUI; it starts an attached backend</span>
<span class="k">$</span> ./meshtalk
<span class="c"># Ctrl+P opens commands</span>
<span class="c"># peers on your LAN appear on the left</span></div>
          <p>MeshTalk keeps its state in <code>~/.meshtalk</code>. Override that location with the <code>MESHTALK_DATA_DIR</code> environment variable.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="remote">
          <h2 class="h3">Remote setup</h2>
          <p>For peers on different networks, point MeshTalk at a control service. Press <code>Ctrl+P</code> in the TUI and choose <strong>Control server</strong>, or set it from the CLI.</p>
          <button class="cmd" type="button" data-copy="meshtalk control set-url wss://control.example/v1/rendezvous">
            <span class="cmd__prompt">$</span>
            <code>meshtalk control set-url wss://control.example/v1/rendezvous</code>
            <span class="cmd__feedback" aria-live="polite" hidden>Copied!</span>
          </button>
          <p>MeshTalk prefers direct UDP and falls back to the relay when direct setup fails. While relayed it keeps probing and switches back once the new path is authenticated. LAN TCP stays the highest-priority path.</p>
          <p><span class="tag">note</span> Production control services must use <code>wss://</code>. Plain <code>ws://</code> is accepted only for localhost development.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="groups">
          <h2 class="h3">Group chats</h2>
          <div class="code-block"><span class="c"># create a named room</span>
<span class="k">$</span> meshtalk room create "Project team"
<span class="g">✓</span> invite copied to clipboard

<span class="c"># share the invite, then on the other device</span>
<span class="k">$</span> meshtalk room join 'meshtalk-group:…'

<span class="c"># send a message</span>
<span class="k">$</span> meshtalk group_send "Project team" "Hello team!"</div>
          <p>Each message is encrypted independently for every member. There is no shared group key, and offline members receive queued delivery when they return.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="docker">
          <h2 class="h3">Docker</h2>
          <div class="code-block"><span class="c"># pull the images</span>
<span class="k">$</span> docker pull ghcr.io/qincai-rui/meshtalk/control:latest
<span class="k">$</span> docker pull ghcr.io/qincai-rui/meshtalk/client:latest

<span class="c"># run the control service</span>
<span class="k">$</span> docker compose up -d

<span class="c"># open a client</span>
<span class="k">$</span> docker compose run -it client</div>
        </section>

        <section id="source">
          <h2 class="h3">Build from source</h2>
          <p>Building needs <a class="link-arrow" href="https://bun.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bun</a> and <a class="link-arrow" href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">uv</a>.</p>
          <div class="code-block"><span class="k">$</span> git clone https://github.com/QinCai-rui/MeshTalk.git
<span class="k">$</span> cd MeshTalk
<span class="k">$</span> bun install
<span class="k">$</span> bun run meshtalk</div>
        </section>

        <section id="update">
          <h2 class="h3">Update</h2>
          <div class="code-block"><span class="c"># update this installation</span>
<span class="k">$</span> ./meshtalk update --install

<span class="c"># update another directory</span>
<span class="k">$</span> ./meshtalk update --install --dir /path/to/MeshTalk</div>
          <p>Updates track <code>QinCai-rui/MeshTalk</code> by default. Point them elsewhere with <code>meshtalk update repo</code>.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
